::CW_U01_L04_Q01::[MLA.CW.WR.03] Passage: "Under the bridge, the river carried leaves, bottle caps, and one red glove. Tomas watched the glove spin away until it became just another bright thing the water had taken." Which paraphrase is most accurate?{
=A. Tomas watches a red glove drift down the river with other objects until it disappears into the moving water.#Correct. This keeps the main action and details.
~B. Tomas throws a glove into the river because he dislikes it.#Incorrect. The passage does not say he throws it.
~C. Tomas collects leaves and bottle caps under a bridge.#Incorrect. The river carries the objects; Tomas watches.
~D. Tomas finds a new glove and takes it home.#Incorrect. The glove moves away in the river.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q02::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "Under the bridge, the river carried leaves, bottle caps, and one red glove. Tomas watched the glove spin away until it became just another bright thing the water had taken." What can the reader infer?{
~A. Tomas is planning to swim after the glove.#Incorrect. The passage does not show him chasing it.
~B. The river is completely empty.#Incorrect. It carries several objects.
=C. The glove may represent loss or something Tomas cannot keep.#Correct. Tomas watches it move away, and the phrase "the water had taken" suggests loss.
~D. The glove belongs to the bridge.#Incorrect. The passage does not support that.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q03::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "Under the bridge, the river carried leaves, bottle caps, and one red glove. Tomas watched the glove spin away until it became just another bright thing the water had taken." Which craft choice makes the glove stand out?{
~A. A direct explanation of Tomas's feelings#Incorrect. Feelings are suggested, not directly explained.
=B. The color detail "red"#Correct. The red glove is visually specific among ordinary debris.
~C. The lack of any image#Incorrect. The passage includes visual images.
~D. A spoken conversation#Incorrect. There is no dialogue.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q04::[MLA.CW.WR.03] Passage: "The ticket stayed in her coat pocket all winter. By spring, the ink had faded, but Leah could still feel the route number pressed into the paper." Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. Leah buys a new ticket in spring because winter ends.#Incorrect. The passage says the ticket stayed all winter.
~B. Leah cannot remember anything about the ticket.#Incorrect. She can still feel the route number.
~C. Leah washes the ticket until it disappears.#Incorrect. The passage says the ink faded, not that she washed it away.
=D. Leah keeps an old ticket for months, and even after the ink fades, she can still feel the number on it.#Correct. This restates the meaning accurately.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q05::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "The ticket stayed in her coat pocket all winter. By spring, the ink had faded, but Leah could still feel the route number pressed into the paper." What inference is best supported?{
=A. The ticket is connected to a memory Leah has not let go.#Correct. Keeping it for months suggests emotional importance.
~B. Leah dislikes travel and avoids buses.#Incorrect. The passage does not support this.
~C. Leah never wears a coat.#Incorrect. The ticket is in her coat pocket.
~D. The ticket has no meaning to Leah.#Incorrect. The repeated contact with the ticket suggests meaning.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q06::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "The ticket stayed in her coat pocket all winter. By spring, the ink had faded, but Leah could still feel the route number pressed into the paper." Which craft move is used?{
~A. A character gives a speech.#Incorrect. No one speaks.
~B. A full argument is directly explained.#Incorrect. The passage suggests meaning indirectly.
=C. An object carries emotional meaning.#Correct. The ticket suggests memory through a physical object.
~D. A recipe is listed.#Incorrect. No recipe appears.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q07::[MLA.CW.WR.03] Passage: "No letter. No call. No light in the upstairs room." Which paraphrase is best?{
~A. The speaker receives a letter and answers a call upstairs.#Incorrect. The passage says the opposite.
=B. The speaker notices several signs of absence: no message, no phone call, and no light upstairs.#Correct. This accurately restates the three missing things.
~C. The upstairs room is bright and crowded.#Incorrect. There is no light.
~D. The speaker writes letters in an upstairs room.#Incorrect. The passage does not say this.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q08::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "No letter. No call. No light in the upstairs room." What craft choice shapes the passage?{
~A. Long flowing sentences#Incorrect. The passage uses fragments.
~B. Dialogue tags#Incorrect. No dialogue appears.
~C. A detailed explanation of history#Incorrect. The passage is compressed.
=D. Repetition of "No"#Correct. The repeated word emphasizes absence.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q09::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "No letter. No call. No light in the upstairs room." What is a reasonable inference?{
=A. The speaker feels disappointed or abandoned.#Correct. The repeated absence suggests emotional disappointment.
~B. The speaker has too many messages.#Incorrect. The passage says there is no letter or call.
~C. A celebration is happening upstairs.#Incorrect. No light suggests absence, not celebration.
~D. The speaker is reading a recipe.#Incorrect. Nothing supports this.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q10::[MLA.CW.WR.03] Passage: "Outside, the parade drums kept shouting. Inside, Elena folded the program into smaller and smaller squares." Which paraphrase is most accurate?{
~A. Elena joins the parade and plays the drums.#Incorrect. Elena is inside folding the program.
~B. The parade is silent and Elena shouts.#Incorrect. The drums are loud, and Elena is quiet.
=C. A loud parade continues outside while Elena quietly folds a program indoors.#Correct. This keeps the contrast between outside and inside.
~D. Elena loses a program before the parade begins.#Incorrect. She is folding the program.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q11::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "Outside, the parade drums kept shouting. Inside, Elena folded the program into smaller and smaller squares." Which craft choice creates meaning?{
~A. A dictionary definition#Incorrect. The passage does not define anything.
=B. Contrast between public noise and private action#Correct. The contrast suggests Elena's inner state differs from the celebration.
~C. A direct statement that Elena is happy#Incorrect. Her emotion is implied.
~D. A list of parade rules#Incorrect. No rules appear.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q12::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "Outside, the parade drums kept shouting. Inside, Elena folded the program into smaller and smaller squares." What can the reader infer about Elena?{
~A. She is leading the parade.#Incorrect. She is inside.
~B. She is writing a newspaper article.#Incorrect. No article appears.
~C. She cannot hear the drums.#Incorrect. The passage presents the drums as part of the scene.
=D. She may feel separate from the celebration.#Correct. Her quiet repetitive action contrasts with the loud parade.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q13::[MLA.CW.WR.03] Passage: "His chair was pushed back from the table, but the napkin still sat folded beside the plate." Which paraphrase is accurate?{
=A. Someone's chair is away from the table while the unused place setting remains.#Correct. This restates the object details accurately.
~B. A person sits and eats a full meal.#Incorrect. The napkin remains folded.
~C. The table is empty with no objects on it.#Incorrect. The napkin and plate are present.
~D. The chair breaks under the table.#Incorrect. The chair is pushed back, not broken.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q14::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "His chair was pushed back from the table, but the napkin still sat folded beside the plate." What craft move appears?{
~A. Direct dialogue#Incorrect. No one speaks.
~B. Rhymed poetry#Incorrect. The sentence is prose and does not rhyme.
=C. Object detail suggesting absence#Correct. The chair and folded napkin suggest someone left or did not eat.
~D. A thesis statement#Incorrect. This is not an essay thesis.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q15::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "His chair was pushed back from the table, but the napkin still sat folded beside the plate." What inference is best supported?{
~A. Everyone finished dinner happily.#Incorrect. The folded napkin suggests the meal was not used.
=B. Someone may have left before eating.#Correct. The pushed-back chair and folded napkin suggest interruption or absence.
~C. The table is outdoors.#Incorrect. The passage does not indicate this.
~D. The plate is missing.#Incorrect. The plate is present.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q16::[MLA.CW.WR.03] Passage: "The kitchen clock clicked through the silence. On the table, two mugs cooled beside a plate no one had touched." Which paraphrase is best?{
~A. A noisy kitchen is full of people eating quickly.#Incorrect. The passage is silent and the plate is untouched.
~B. Someone repairs a kitchen clock while cooking.#Incorrect. No repair or cooking appears.
~C. Two mugs break beside a plate.#Incorrect. The mugs cool; they do not break.
=D. A quiet kitchen contains cooling mugs and untouched food, suggesting no one is speaking or eating.#Correct. This preserves both literal details and implied situation.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q17::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "The kitchen clock clicked through the silence. On the table, two mugs cooled beside a plate no one had touched." Which detail creates sound imagery?{
=A. clock clicked#Correct. This appeals to hearing.
~B. two mugs#Incorrect. This is visual object detail.
~C. plate#Incorrect. The plate itself does not create sound.
~D. table#Incorrect. The table is a setting object.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q18::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "The kitchen clock clicked through the silence. On the table, two mugs cooled beside a plate no one had touched." What can the reader infer?{
~A. Everyone is loudly celebrating.#Incorrect. The passage emphasizes silence.
~B. The food has just been served and eaten.#Incorrect. The plate is untouched.
=C. There may be tension or absence in the room.#Correct. Silence and untouched food suggest something unresolved.
~D. The clock has disappeared.#Incorrect. The clock is present and clicking.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q19::[MLA.CW.WR.03] Passage: "At the curb, the suitcase waited beside her like a quiet accusation. Nina kept one hand on the handle and one eye on the upstairs window. No curtain moved. No voice called her back." Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. Nina opens the upstairs curtain and calls to someone.#Incorrect. No curtain moves, and no voice calls.
=B. Nina waits outside with a suitcase, watching the window, but no one responds.#Correct. This restates the scene accurately.
~C. Nina leaves the suitcase in a car and forgets it.#Incorrect. Her hand is on the handle.
~D. Nina celebrates with people upstairs.#Incorrect. The passage suggests silence and tension.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q20::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "At the curb, the suitcase waited beside her like a quiet accusation. Nina kept one hand on the handle and one eye on the upstairs window. No curtain moved. No voice called her back." Which craft choice makes the suitcase meaningful?{
~A. A joke about luggage#Incorrect. The tone is serious, not comic.
~B. A list of travel rules#Incorrect. No rules appear.
~C. A direct explanation of every event#Incorrect. The passage leaves meaning implied.
=D. The simile "like a quiet accusation"#Correct. The comparison suggests guilt or conflict.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q21::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "At the curb, the suitcase waited beside her like a quiet accusation. Nina kept one hand on the handle and one eye on the upstairs window. No curtain moved. No voice called her back." What can the reader infer?{
=A. Nina may be leaving after conflict or waiting to be stopped.#Correct. The suitcase, window, and silence support this inference.
~B. Nina is buying a suitcase online.#Incorrect. Nothing supports online shopping.
~C. Nina cannot see the upstairs window.#Incorrect. She keeps one eye on it.
~D. Nina hears many voices calling.#Incorrect. No voice calls her back.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q22::[MLA.CW.WR.03] Passage: "The notebook opened to a page of crossed-out names. Only one name remained, circled so many times the paper had nearly torn." Which paraphrase is most accurate?{
~A. The notebook is blank and unused.#Incorrect. The page contains crossed-out and circled names.
~B. The paper is new and untouched.#Incorrect. It is nearly torn.
=C. A notebook page shows many crossed-out names and one heavily circled name.#Correct. This keeps the important visual details.
~D. A character reads a printed newspaper.#Incorrect. The passage is about a notebook page.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q23::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "The notebook opened to a page of crossed-out names. Only one name remained, circled so many times the paper had nearly torn." What craft move does the model use?{
~A. Direct explanation of a theme#Incorrect. The passage implies meaning through objects.
=B. Visual detail to suggest obsession or focus#Correct. Crossed-out names and repeated circles show intense attention.
~C. A cheerful song#Incorrect. No song appears.
~D. A formal citation#Incorrect. No source is cited.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q24::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "The notebook opened to a page of crossed-out names. Only one name remained, circled so many times the paper had nearly torn." What is a reasonable inference?{
~A. The notebook has never been used.#Incorrect. It has many marks.
~B. The names are printed on a ticket.#Incorrect. The passage says notebook.
~C. The paper cannot tear.#Incorrect. It has nearly torn.
=D. The remaining name matters strongly to someone.#Correct. The repeated circling suggests intense focus or emotion.
}
::CW_U01_L04_Q25::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "The notebook opened to a page of crossed-out names. Only one name remained, circled so many times the paper had nearly torn." Which response best reads the passage as a craft model?{
=A. The writer uses marked-up object detail to suggest emotion without directly explaining it.#Correct. This identifies the craft move and its effect.
~B. The writer gives no details.#Incorrect. The notebook page details are specific.
~C. The writer explains everything directly.#Incorrect. Meaning is implied through the page.
~D. The writer only teaches spelling.#Incorrect. The passage is a craft model, not a spelling lesson.
}
